    {
      "answer": "According to the context passages, materials that can be added to a compost heap include:\n\n* Kitchen scraps (including meat, grass, hedge clippings, eggshells, etc.)\n* Garden waste\n* Leaves\n* Weeds (including nasty ones like docks, dandelions, and scutch)\n* Sawdust\n* Straw\n* Cardboard\n* Grass\n* Hedge clippings\n* Eggshells\n* Meat (in moderation, with aeration to allow for quick breakdown)\n* Farmyard manure\n* Spent mushroom compost\n* Vegetable grower's compost\n* Woody prunings\n* Partially rotted leaves\n* Straw\n* Brussels sprout stems (chopped up)\n* Small particles (with ideal size range of 12-40 mm screen size)\n* Clay soil (up to 10% can be added to the heap)\n* Wet materials (e.g. grass, which is about 83% water)\n* Wet straw (if adding straw)\n\nNote that some materials should be avoided, such as diseased plants (except for certain exceptions like potato blight), and materials that may attract pests (like rats and mice).",
